Description: v4l2: count a stateful decoder instance exactly once
 The stateful decoder limits how many hardware decoders exist at once with
 a static counter. Initialize() increments it speculatively and undoes that
 on refusal, but Reset() and the destructor decrement unconditionally. A
 decoder that was refused, or never initialised, still gives a slot back
 when destroyed; one that is Reset() and then destroyed gives its slot back
 twice; one re-initialised for a track change takes a second slot that is
 only returned when it is reset or destroyed, so with our bcm2835 limit of
 two (v4l2-stateful-limit-instances.patch; upstream's 15 only hides the
 drift longer) one track change leaves that video holding both slots, and
 at the limit the track change fails outright with kTooManyDecoders. Each
 spurious decrement permanently raises the effective limit by one, so a
 page with several videos erodes the limit within minutes.
 .
 On a Pi 4 a third 1080p instance runs the VideoCore's relocatable heap out
 (alloc_compact_internal failures in vclog), after which the firmware stops
 answering and the next port disable times out: the signature reported in
 trixie-feedback#101. We reproduced the counter drift and that hang with
 four <video> elements and a page reload, and the same hang with three
 standalone V4L2 clients and no Chromium at all. This patch removes
 Chromium's way of getting there; the firmware's reaction to running out of
 memory is a separate bug.
 .
 Remember per object whether it holds a slot, take one only when it does
 not, and release it exactly once, in Reset() or in the destructor. Reset()
 closes the device and Decode() then re-runs Initialize(), which takes a
 slot again. The owner destroys a decoder whose Initialize() failed, so no
 separate release is needed there. The flag has to survive that failure:
 an Initialize() that fails after opening the device leaves the fd open, so
 the slot must stay held, and Decode() would retry Initialize() on the same
 object. Every release is guarded by the per-object flag, as
 V4L2VideoDecoder does with can_use_decoder_, except that a re-Initialize()
 for a track change keeps the slot rather than releasing and re-taking it,
 which is what stops that track change failing at the limit. The pipeline's
 own DecoderReservation is private to it and hard-coded to a different
 limit, so it cannot be reused here.
 .
 Known and deliberately not addressed. The destructor releases the slot
 before the posted close of the device fd runs, so a new decoder can briefly
 hold a second fd on the device; when teardown succeeds both queues are
 destroyed synchronously first, and an open fd with no buffers does not hold
 the VPU memory the limit exists to protect (a loop of open/close alongside
 two 1080p decoders leaves the relocatable heap untouched). Reset() likewise
 releases the slot that the following Decode() re-takes, so a decoder that
 seeks can lose its slot to another one and fail with kTooManyDecoders,
 which is fatal once it has produced a frame; the window is upstream's, but
 it only becomes reachable now that the limit binds, so seeking on a
 multi-video page wants testing. Finally GetMaxNumDecoderInstances() returns
 INT_MAX if it cannot open the device, and the result is cached for the life
 of the process, so one transient failure still disables the limit
 entirely.
 .
 Upstream main has the same code. The destructor decrement has been
 unconditional since the counter arrived in crrev.com/c/5368833; c/5474676
 later re-added the Reset() decrement (first added by c/5423008, dropped by
 c/5436089), also unconditional. Not forwarded yet; a crbug and CL should
 follow.
